      <h2 class="section-title mb-12">The four-stage framework</h2>
      <div class="process-grid">
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>01</span>
          <h3>Assess</h3>
          <p>We audit your current infrastructure against operational requirements, regulatory obligations, and growth plans. We identify gaps, risks, and opportunities — documented, prioritised, and costed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>02</span>
          <h3>Design</h3>
          <p>We lock the architecture, vendor selection, and budget into one executable plan. Engineering diagrams, BoQs, and operational runbooks are signed off before procurement begins.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>03</span>
          <h3>Build</h3>
          <p>We coordinate engineering, procurement, and commissioning with clear milestones and quality gates. You see progress against a plan, not a wish list.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>04</span>
          <h3>Operate</h3>
          <p>We monitor, patch, and respond through 24/7 NOC and SOC operations. The same team that built it runs it — no knowledge transfer gaps.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section-alt reveal">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 lg:px-6">
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Why it matters</p>
          <h2 class="section-title">One framework. No gaps.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose-content">
          <p>Most infrastructure projects fail at the handoffs — between consultant and integrator, between integrator and operator, between vendor and client. Our four-stage framework eliminates those handoffs because the same team owns every stage.</p>
          <p>The team that assesses your environment designs the solution. The team that designs it builds it. The team that builds it operates it. That continuity means fewer surprises, faster resolution, and infrastructure that actually works the way it was drawn.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

